// Package db is the LoGOs twin of the scanning path of database/sql.
//
// It provides the typed row path without the per-column reflection of
// convertAssignRows: Assign copies one driver value into a typed address
// with a comptime-selected converter, and Query and One scan whole rows
// into structs or scalars by expanding fields at compile time. The only
// run-time dispatch left is a type switch on the driver value, which is
// boxed by the driver before the twin ever sees it.
//
// The hot path never calls reflect: every question about the destination
// type is answered by a const switch or a const for over struct fields.
// The two run-time type assertions that remain are on values whose type
// is known only at run time: a destination that implements sql.Scanner
// (for example sql.Null) and the *any destination.
//
// Compatibility boundary: Assign covers the destination types the
// conversion table pins (string, bool, the int, uint and float kinds and
// their named types, []byte, time.Time, uuid.UUID, RawBytes as an owned
// copy, *any, and any sql.Scanner). Deliberately absent, each with the
// reason: pointer destinations (a NULL maps to an absent option field,
// never to a nil pointer; use T?); named slice types other than the
// exact []byte (a const switch tilde case needs a basic type per SPEC
// T-205, so there is no compile-time form for them); decimal compose
// targets and driver cursor destinations (both interfaces are
// unexported, so no twin code can name them); driver argument
// conversion (Valuer, ColumnConverter, NamedValueChecker), which stays
// in database/sql and database/sql/driver. Query materializes every row,
// so a RawBytes field holds an owned copy, not pinned driver memory;
// the zero-allocation pin of rows.rawbuf is a database/sql internal and
// cannot be named from outside the package.
//
// Error contract: plain errors whose texts keep the legacy "db: " cause
// prefix the spira consumer matches on (column count and name, NULL in
// a non-option field, more than one row for One, unsupported type, scan
// wrap). The twin carries no fault codes and no dialect classification;
// per-column failures additionally carry the standard library wrap
// "sql: Scan error on column index %d, name %q" around the twin text,
// because the conversion runs inside rows.Scan.
//
// Allocation bound: zero twin-side allocations per scanned column for
// scalar destinations; one allocation exactly where a new value must be
// materialized (a formatted number string, a cloned byte slice). The
// destination vector is built once per query and reused for every row,
// unlike a per-row rebuild. Dynamic Scanner destinations may allocate
// exactly as the standard library does.
package db
